::scene PICTURE

LETTERBOX on

TITLE captain_quorum_title

♪ A brass fanfare in municipal green. Six bars. It will be back. ♪

SHOT
The print is immaculate. That is the first thing anybody notices and the last thing anybody forgets: eighty years on, this stock is better than anything the basin has made since, and the greens are still green.
SHOT_END

SLUG THE CIVIC HALL | THE YEAR OF THE THIRD RESERVOIR

SHOT
Bunting. A dais. Two hundred citizens in the seats, and the extras have all been directed to face front and not to move their heads.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
The Directorate produced eleven of these. Nine are lost. This is not one of the good ones.

ACT 1: The Chair Recognises

SHOT
CAPTAIN QUORUM enters to applause that starts a half-second before he appears.
SHOT_END

QUORUM:
Friends! Neighbours! Ratepayers! The business before us is SHORT and the afternoon is LONG!

SHOT
The crowd laughs on cue. Some of them are a beat late and you can see which ones.
SHOT_END

CLERK:
A written amendment, Captain. From the gentleman in the third row.

QUORUM:
An AMENDMENT! Splendid! The system WORKS!

SLUG THE THIRD ROW | ONE MOMENT LATER

SHOT
A man stands up. He is the only person in the hall shot in close-up, which in a Directorate picture is not a compliment.
SHOT_END

AMENDER:
I move that the meter clause be struck.

QUORUM:
The gentleman moves that the meter clause be struck! Is the motion SECONDED?

SHOT
A long pan across two hundred people, none of whom move.
SHOT_END

QUORUM:
The motion FAILS for want of a second! Next business!

NARRATOR:
Twenty-one seconds. There is a stopwatch on the print and the Directorate was proud of it.

ACT 2: The Gentleman Persists

SLUG THE CIVIC HALL | THE FOLLOWING WEEK

SHOT
The same hall. The same bunting. The same man, standing up again.
SHOT_END

AMENDER:
I move that the meter clause be struck.

QUORUM:
Is the motion SECONDED?

SHOT
Nobody moves. Except, at the very back of frame, in the corner, out of focus, somebody's hand.
SHOT_END

♪ The fanfare tries to start and stops. ♪

QUORUM:
... I'm sorry?

SLUG THE CIVIC HALL | THE WEEK AFTER THAT

SHOT
Four hands.
SHOT_END

SLUG THE CIVIC HALL | THE WEEK AFTER THAT

SHOT
Forty.
SHOT_END

TECH_DIFFICULTIES 6

SHOT
REEL TWO IS DAMAGED on every surviving print, always at the same place, which the collectors' consensus holds is not a coincidence and the archivists' consensus holds is a bad splice. Both parties have written at length.
SHOT_END

INTERMISSION 40

ACT 3: The Motion Carries

SLUG THE CIVIC HALL | LATE

SHOT
The hall is dark except for the dais. QUORUM is alone at the lectern with the gavel in front of him and does not pick it up.
SHOT_END

QUORUM:
Clerk. How many.

CLERK:
All of them, Captain.

QUORUM:
That isn't a number.

CLERK:
No, Captain.

SHOT
He straightens his sash. The costume department made four of those and this is the last one, and it is visibly the last one.
SHOT_END

QUORUM:
Then the motion is seconded.

QUORUM:
And the motion is DEBATED.

QUORUM:
And the motion, having been debated —

SHOT
He picks up the gavel. The Directorate's animation unit put a starburst behind this shot and it is the single most expensive frame in the picture.
SHOT_END

QUORUM:
— CARRIES.

♪ The fanfare, at last, all six bars, and about a tone flat. ♪

FADE out 3

SLUG THE CIVIC HALL | THE FOLLOWING SPRING

SHOT
An empty hall with the chairs stacked. On the far wall, low, half hidden behind them: a small painted-over cabinet with a little glass window.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
The meter clause was struck.

NARRATOR:
The meters stayed.

FADE in 2

LETTERBOX off

